PRE-REGISTRATION -- cross-agent determinism arm            2026-09-12
Written BEFORE any Claude Code determinism run.

WHY THIS AXIS, WHEN TASK SUCCESS IS EXHAUSTED
  The parity question has TWO blockers (ADR-0151, rb-10756): saturation, and the
  model confound. Three measured attempts closed the saturation route -- both
  arms pass everything, including three tasks purpose-built to reject wrong
  answers (ADR-0156). The model confound looked equally fatal because holding
  the model fixed is impossible on this box.
  IT IS NOT FATAL ON THIS AXIS. Determinism is a property of the LOOP, not of
  the model: each agent runs its own model and is measured on RUN-TO-RUN
  VARIANCE of its own outputs. Nothing is compared across models, so nothing is
  confounded by them. This is also the user's redirect stated literally --
  "more deterministic" -- and it is the first axis in this campaign where
  zakcode could be measurably BETTER rather than merely equal.

WHAT IS ALREADY MEASURED (zakcode side, ADR-0152)
  With ZBENCH_PIN_IDENTITY set, m05 at cap=64 reproduced 3/3 EXACTLY: 22
  iterations every run, identical stop-reason histogram {reasoning_overflow: 4,
  stuck: 1}, wall clock 61.9 / 62.0 / 62.0s. Byte-identical outputs.
  The two causes of residual nondeterminism were found and are NOT the engine:
  a uuid4 session id and an mkdtemp suffix reaching the system prompt, and the
  task's own pytest wall-clock duration ("10 passed in 0.01s" vs "0.02s").
  Reproduction is BATCH-LOCAL: identical config reproduces exactly within a
  batch, not necessarily across batches.

WHAT IS NOT MEASURED (the gap this closes)
  Claude Code's run-to-run variance on the same task. NOTHING in this campaign
  has measured it. Without it, "zakcode is more deterministic" is an assertion.

DESIGN
  Task: 02-median-bug -- the cheapest task ($0.1531, 7 turns, 7.1s), so N runs
  are affordable and fast. N = 3, matching ADR-0152's zakcode N.
  Each run: identical prompt, identical seed workspace, identical --allowedTools,
  fresh temp workspace. Record per run: verify_rc, num_turns, total_cost_usd,
  elapsed_s, and the SHA-256 of every file the agent left in the workspace.
  The file digest is the real measurement -- turns and cost are coarse proxies
  and can coincide while the produced code differs.

PREDICTIONS, fixed before data
  P1 Claude Code will NOT be byte-identical across the 3 runs. Its sampling is
     not pinned, and no identity-pinning equivalent to ZBENCH_PIN_IDENTITY was
     applied. I expect differing file digests on at least 2 of 3.
  P2 It WILL be outcome-stable: 3/3 verify_rc == 0. Correctness is not the
     variable here.
  P3 num_turns will vary by at least 1 across the 3 runs.

DECISION RULES
  CC digests differ AND zakcode's are identical -> zakcode is measurably MORE
     DETERMINISTIC than the reference agent on the same task shape. That is a
     parity-relevant claim that is NOT model-confounded, and the first one this
     campaign can make in zakcode's favour.
  CC digests identical across 3 -> the reference agent is also deterministic
     here; the claimed advantage does not exist at N=3 and must be withdrawn,
     not restated with a larger N chosen after the fact.
  MIXED (some identical, some not) -> report the counts, claim nothing stronger
     than the counts, and state N=3 is too small to resolve a rate.

HONESTY CONSTRAINT
  zakcode's 3/3 was measured WITH identity pinning, which is a deliberate
  intervention. Claude Code gets no equivalent because none exists to apply.
  So the comparison is "zakcode with its determinism feature ON" vs "Claude Code
  as it ships". That is the HONEST framing and it must be stated with the
  result -- it is a claim about an available capability, not about the two loops
  under identical treatment.
